claimed: intent-language-gap — Russian 0.652 and German 0.813 against Spanish 0.914 and French 0.900, with domain-bearing names and no gate, so this is not a gate artifact and not a simple English-first ordering (English 0.881 sits below Spanish and French). Two candidates, measured SEPARATELY because they cost different things: case names written in the message language, and an explicit domain sentence in the prompt. The precondition first, as the entry demanded: grow the parallel set from 12 meanings to 30 in all six languages, weighted toward the out-of-domain boundary where the classifier actually breaks. The author-written-translation limitation does not go away with more rows and will be restated rather than quietly outgrown.
